plain    'Attack at dawn, żółw!'
rot13    'Nggnpx ng qnja, żółj!'
rot13^2  'Attack at dawn, żółw!'   -- back to the start

Applying it twice returns the input, because 13 + 13 = 26 and the
alphabet is 26 long. That is not a property of encryption, it is a
property of half. The shift is in the name, so the keyspace has one
element and there is nothing to guess.

THE LETTERS IT LEFT ALONE -- and the one it did not
   'w' U+0077  -> 'j'   ASCII, so it rotates like any other letter
   'ż' U+017C  unchanged -- outside a..z and A..Z
   'ó' U+00F3  unchanged -- outside a..z and A..Z
   'ł' U+0142  unchanged -- outside a..z and A..Z

The two things that make rot13 feel elegant are facts about ASCII's
layout, not about rotation:
  * it is ONE SUM, because the 26 letters are contiguous. In a table
    where they are not, the same idea needs a lookup.
  * the byte count never moves, because every ASCII letter is one byte
    and stays one byte.

Rotate past ASCII and you lose both. Add 13 to the code point instead:
   'ż' + 13 = 'Ɖ'   2 byte(s) -> 2
   'ó' + 13 = 'Ā'   2 byte(s) -> 2
   'ł' + 13 = 'ŏ'   2 byte(s) -> 2
   Not letters, not a rotation, and nothing came back round -- because
   there is no 26-long run to come round in.

Python's own codec agrees on the ASCII part: 'Nggnpx'
